<section>
  <h1>
    You've somehow found the unofficial FN web components library. Good for
    you.
  </h1><br />
  This is all about <a
    href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components"
    target="_blank">web components</a
  > and simplicity. Styling is based on <a
    href="https://rex.rakuten.design/en/design/components/"
    target="_blank">Fn Guidelines</a
  > where possible and extra functionality is kept to a minimum. You are encrouaged
  download each component individually and customise it to your specific needs as
  you see fit.<br />

  <h3>This means <em>NO NPM INSTALL</em>. Why?</h3>
  When you install a library via NPM what happens? It gets thrown into the node_modules
  folder and you import it into your code from there. Which means your options for
  modifications are limited. You cannot modify what is inside node_modules because
  for one you usually do not publish that folder and two, what happens when that
  library updates? All your modified code is gone.
  <br /><br />
  This encrouages developers to tell designers that they need to only include in
  designs what the developers chosen library can do, limiting both functional and
  visual design.
  <br /><br />
  This library of web components is specifically designed to be modified and altered.
  Download each component.js file and rewrite the CSS, alter the JavaScript and HTML
  to do what your specific design calls for.
  <br /><br />

  Repo: <a
    target="_blank"
    href="https://ghe.rakuten-it.com/ssed/fn-web-components/"
    >https://ghe.rakuten-it.com/ssed/fn-web-components/</a
  >
</section>
