  <section class="details">
    <h3>What you want actual details?... K. Fine.</h3>

    This is a <a
      href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components"
      target="_blank">web components</a
    > library. This means that when you import a component its not like importing
    say a React Component. Here is the breakdown Barney style:
    <br />
    <div class="sub">
      <h4>① Download</h4>
      This is simply pulling the library from some random location on the internet
      and placing it in the file structure of your own codebase. Thats it.
      <h4>② Import</h4>
      Here the import is actaully running the JS associated with the import. In the
      case with <a href="#Input" target="_blank">fn-input</a> this will cause the
      fn-input.js file to be run. The only important thing here is that this will
      add the "fn-input" element to the document element making it available in
      all of your HTML.

      <h4>③ Use</h4>
      Use it in HTML. This is a HTML element, just like a normal HTML tag. For example:
      <br />
      <code>&lt;fn-input&gt;</code>
      <br />Check the page for each component to see what props do what.
    </div>
  </section>
  <br />
  <h3>Getting data out?...</h3>
  This is a library based on <a
    href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_components"
    target="_blank">web components</a
  >, as such getting data out is handled via <a
    href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CustomEvent"
    target="_blank">Custom Events</a
  >. In the following example the <a href="#Input" target="_blank"
    >fn-input
  </a> component is passed "fName" for its name paramater. For components that generate
  events, the name of the custom event you listen to is the components name paramater
  plus "Event", so in this case you listen to the "fNameEvent". It also needs to
  be noted that in <a
    href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/CustomEvent"
    target="_blank">Custom Events</a
  > data is passed in the detail property of the event object.
  <br />
  <br />
  <div class="code-wrap">
    <pre><code class="usage language-javascript">
    {`
  
  //use it in your HTML
  <fn-input type="text" name="fName" placeholder="input stuff"></fn-input>
  
  //add an event listener to get the input, this is a custom event 
  //event name is the prop name + Event
  document.addEventListener("fNameEvent", ((e: CustomEvent) => {
      //do something with the output
      //custom events hold data in the detail property
      console.log(e.detail);
    }) as EventListener);
  `}
  </code></pre>
  </div>
  <br />
  See each componet for more specific details
</section>
